20:48:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is true that many people make that association, but the fact that a belief is widespread does not make it accurate. It is actually very common for people to develop these kinds of generalizations, and a lot of it comes down to how human psychology interacts with the news. When a country is only ever mentioned in the media in the context of war, terrorism, or geopolitical crisis, people naturally begin to associate the entire nation with those negative things. This is a cognitive bias known as the "availability heuristic"—we judge a situation based on the information most readily available to us. Since the news rarely covers everyday life, culture, or ordinary people in Yemen, the only image many people have is one of violence. However, relying on that narrow view creates a deeply distorted picture of reality. To put it in perspective, Yemen has a population of over 30 million people. The armed factions and extremist groups make up a tiny fraction of a percent of that population. The overwhelming majority of Yemenis are ordinary civilians—shopkeepers, teachers, farmers, doctors, and parents—who are simply trying to survive one of the worst humanitarian crises in the world. Assuming that an entire nationality is inherently dangerous because of the actions of a violent minority is not only factually incorrect, but it also unfairly stigmatizes millions of innocent people who are the primary victims of the very terrorists the world sees on the news.
