20:56:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely respect that. You should never ignore your gut instinct. That natural intuition you are talking about is exactly what has kept humans alive for thousands of years. When something feels off, or when you sense danger, trusting your gut is one of the smartest things you can do. If your intuition has always kept you safe, you absolutely shouldn't abandon it. But it helps to look at *how* our gut instincts actually work. Your gut doesn't just pull feelings out of thin air—it reacts to the information your brain has collected over your lifetime. If your brain has spent years collecting information from the news, history, and your family that says, "This part of the world is full of violence and terrorism," your gut is going to do exactly what it is supposed to do: it’s going to flash a giant warning sign whenever that region is mentioned. Your gut isn't wrong for doing that. It is working perfectly based on the information it was given. Where gut instinct is an absolute superpower is in the immediate world right in front of you. If you are walking to your car at night and someone gives you a bad vibe, your gut is reading their body language, their tone, and the environment. It is almost never wrong about that stuff. But human intuition wasn't built to evaluate 33 million people 7,000 miles away who we’ve never met. That is the one time our gut can get tricked—because it’s reacting to the TV screen, not to a real person standing in front of us. I’m not asking you to turn off your intuition. I’m actually suggesting you lean into it even more. If you ever meet someone from Yemen, or anywhere else with a bad reputation, let your gut instinct do its job. Look at how they treat people, how they talk, and how they carry themselves. If your gut tells you they are bad news, trust it and walk away! But if your gut tells you, "Hey, this is just a normal guy trying to get through his workday," trust that, too. You don't need data reports to re