m over 80 different countries traveled to Syria to join these groups. They exploited the country's lack of borders and security to set up their camps. When you look at these facts—that the local population fought against the extremists, that millions fled in terror from them, and that the country was invaded by foreign fighters—it becomes clear why security analysts do not view terrorism as a trait supported by the Syrian people. Saying "Syrians are generally terrorists" takes the horrific actions of a radical militant group and blames it on the very people who were their primary victims. I understand that the sheer volume of news coverage about Syria and terrorism makes it feel like the two are deeply intertwined. But looking at the actual data, the Syrian people as a group did not support the terrorism that destroyed their country. We can absolutely agree that Syria is currently a highly dangerous place, and that the region has serious security challenges. But if your friend is traveling to a stable Middle Eastern country, she will be surrounded by the mainstream culture of a region that has actively fought against and rejected the extremism you are worried about.
